
========================================================================================
THE HESSIAN NO-GO (Carl's sharpest result): fixed quadratic gradient => no nonlinear mu
========================================================================================
  exact branch: L_grad = (A/2N)(D phi)^2.  dL/d(Dphi^2) = A/(2*N) = A/2N, CONSTANT in D phi.
  => Hessian d^2L/d(D_i phi)d(D_j phi) = (A/N) h^ij, INDEPENDENT of D phi.
  => the elliptic phi-equation D_i[(A/N) D^i phi + ...] is LINEAR in D phi.
  => CANNOT be D_i[mu(g/a0) D^i phi] with mu'(x) != 0.  MOND is impossible here.

========================================================================================
what nonlinear MOND REQUIRES
========================================================================================
  L_grad = a0^2 U(X), X=(Dphi)^2/a0^2:
     Hessian = 2 U_X h_ij + (4/a0^2) U_XX (D_i phi)(D_j phi).
  The (D_i phi)(D_j phi) piece -- the source of nonlinear mu -- is present iff U_XX != 0.
  So: nonlinear MOND  <=>  U_XX != 0  <=>  OUTSIDE the exact-d=2 quadratic branch.

========================================================================================
THE BINARY that decides the whole program (quartic order, A1/A2 cubic branch)
========================================================================================
  The exact d=2 branch is RULED OUT for MOND (Hessian is fixed quadratic).  Only the
  A1/A2 GR-compatible branches of the CUBIC construction survive, and 2-DOF there is proven
  only THROUGH CUBIC ORDER.  Put the MOND nonlinearity in an auxiliary spatial potential
  U((D phi)^2/a0(q)^2) and solve the QUARTIC degeneracy conditions.  Two outcomes, binary:
     quartic forces U_XX = 0   =>  NO-GO THEOREM: GR-compatible 2-DOF SCG cannot generate
                                    nonlinear MOND through this auxiliary scalar. [publishable]
     quartic permits U_XX != 0 =>  an explicit nonlinear 2-DOF MOND Lagrangian. [the target]
  a0(q)=cq/Z, K=q => a0(z)~H(z) rides along either way.  This is the calculation with a
  definite yes/no answer -- no more decorating the exact branch.
